course: space
lesson: 23
title: The Milky Way
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Sun is not alone: it is one of a few hundred billion stars in a giant spinning disc called the Milky Way, our galaxy.
explain: We live inside the disc, so when we look along it we see a pale band of countless stars stretching across a dark sky.
explain: The Milky Way is shaped like two fried eggs back to back with a bar of stars across the yolk and arms curling out like a pinwheel.
explain: We sit far out in a small arm, about 26,000 light years from the busy centre, where a giant black hole hides.
explain: Out camping on a dark night, with no street lights, you can see this milky river; in most towns light pollution has washed it away.
fact: The Milky Way is our galaxy, a huge disc of a few hundred billion stars, and the Sun is just one of them.
fact: The Milky Way is a barred spiral galaxy, with a bar of stars across its centre and spiral arms curling out from it.
fact: The disc of the Milky Way is about 100,000 light years across but only about 1,000 light years thick in most places.
fact: The Sun lies about 26,000 light years from the galaxy's centre, in a smaller arm called the Orion Spur.
fact: The Sun takes about 230 million years to go once round the centre of the galaxy, a "galactic year".
fact: At the galaxy's centre is a black hole, a place where gravity is so strong that not even light can escape, called Sagittarius A*, about 4 million times the mass of the Sun, first imaged in 2022.
fact: The word galaxy comes from the Greek word for milk, and the Milky Way was named for its pale milky look.
fact: In 1610 Galileo turned a small telescope on the Milky Way and found it was made of countless faint stars.
fact: Most people in Britain, the USA and Europe can no longer see the Milky Way from home because of light pollution, the glow of street lights and buildings in the night sky.
fact: The Large and Small Magellanic Clouds are two small galaxies near ours, seen as misty patches in the southern sky.
fact: The Milky Way and the Andromeda Galaxy are approaching each other and may merge in several billion years.
fact: A 2025 study put the chance of a Milky Way and Andromeda collision within 10 billion years at only about even, so this is uncertain.
exercise: What is the Milky Way? | Our galaxy, a huge disc of stars | A cloud of gas around the Earth | A group of planets around the Sun | A single very bright star
exercise: About how many stars are in the Milky Way? | A few hundred billion | A few thousand | About nine | About one million
exercise: What kind of galaxy is the Milky Way? | A barred spiral | A round elliptical | A shapeless irregular | A flat ring
exercise: About how wide is the disc of the Milky Way? | About 100,000 light years | About 100 light years | About 1,000 kilometres | About 4 light years
exercise: How thick is the disc in most places? | About 1,000 light years | About 100,000 light years | About 1 light year | About as thick as it is wide
exercise: Where is the Sun in the galaxy? | About 26,000 light years from the centre, in the Orion Spur | Right at the very centre | At the very edge of the galaxy, where the last few stars thin out | Outside the galaxy altogether
exercise: How long does the Sun take to go once round the galaxy? | About 230 million years | About one year | About 230 years | About 26,000 years
exercise: What is at the centre of the Milky Way? | A black hole called Sagittarius A* | A giant sun bigger than all the other stars | Nothing but empty space, with the stars going round it | The planet Jupiter, which pulls the stars round
exercise: When was Sagittarius A* first imaged? | In 2022 | In 1610 | In 1969 | It has never been imaged
exercise: Where does the word galaxy come from? | The Greek word for milk | The Latin word for star | The Greek word for wheel | The English word for gas
exercise: What did Galileo find in 1610 when he looked at the Milky Way? | It was made of countless faint stars | It was a cloud of spilled milk | It was a ring of ice | It was a river of gas
exercise: Why can most people in Britain not see the Milky Way from home? | Light pollution from street lights and buildings | The Milky Way only shows from Australia and the far south | The Milky Way has faded since 1900 | British skies are too close to the Sun
exercise: What are the Magellanic Clouds? | Two small galaxies near ours | Two rain clouds over Chile | Two bright planets | Two parts of the Milky Way's bar
exercise: What may happen to the Milky Way and Andromeda in billions of years? | They may merge | They will certainly swap places | They will turn into one star | They will both disappear
exercise: How sure are scientists that the Milky Way and Andromeda will collide? | Not sure: a 2025 study put it at only about even odds | Completely sure, it will definitely happen next century | Sure it can never happen | Sure it already happened long ago
test: Tom says "the Milky Way is a chocolate bar, not a real thing in space". What should you tell him? | It is our real galaxy, and the bar is named after it | He is right, it is only a sweet that was named after a star | It is just a thin cloud of dust floating around the Earth | It is another, older name for the Moon and its pale light
test: Why does the Milky Way look like a band across the sky, not a round blob? | We are inside a flat disc and look along it | The galaxy is a long thin tube | The Earth's air stretches the round galaxy into a band | Only one row of stars exists
test: If you could watch from far outside, which picture would show the Milky Way? | A flat pinwheel with a bar in the middle | A round ball of stars | A long straight line of stars | A ring with nothing inside
test: How many times has the Sun gone round the galaxy since the dinosaurs died out 66 million years ago? | Less than once | About 66 times | Thousands of times | It does not move at all
test: camping in the Highlands n there's this pale milky stripe across the sky. what is it | The Milky Way, our galaxy seen from inside | A long white trail left behind by an aeroplane | Mist rising slowly from the hills and the lochs | Light glowing up from a far-off city like Glasgow
test: In a game the Sun sits right in the middle of the galaxy. What is wrong? | The Sun is far out, about 26,000 light years from the centre | The Sun is at the very outer edge | The Sun is outside the galaxy | Nothing, the Sun really does sit right in the middle of the galaxy
test: Which is true about the black hole at the centre of our galaxy? | It is about 4 million times the mass of the Sun | It is about the size and mass of the Earth, only darker | It will swallow the Earth some time next year, bit by bit | It is only a myth, and there is no evidence for it at all
test: Mia in Melbourne sees two misty patches far from the Milky Way's band. What are they likely to be? | The Magellanic Clouds, small galaxies | Two comets that never move | Two clouds of smoke from fires | Dust on her telescope
test: Your nan says she could see the Milky Way from her garden as a girl but you can't today. Most likely why? | More light pollution now | The Milky Way has moved away | Her eyes were special | Stars were brighter in the past
test: News says "the Milky Way WILL crash into Andromeda". What is the fairer way to put it? | They might merge in billions of years, but it is uncertain | It will certainly happen within our own lifetime, maybe soon | It can never happen at all | It already happened last century
test: Why did people before Galileo not know the Milky Way was made of stars? | The stars are too faint to see one by one without a telescope | Nobody ever looked up at night | The stars were not there before 1610 | They thought the faint stars were only planets, not far-off suns
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the structure of the Milky Way? | It is a barred spiral galaxy, a disc of a few hundred billion stars | It is an elliptical galaxy of a few thousand stars | It is a spherical cloud of gas which surrounds the Earth and the Moon | It is an irregular cluster of nine planets
exam: Ascertain the approximate distance of the Sun from the centre of the galaxy. | About twenty-six thousand light years | About one hundred light years | About one astronomical unit | About one hundred thousand light years
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the Milky Way is no longer visible from most homes in Britain and the USA. | Light pollution | Atmospheric dust from volcanoes | The recession of the galaxy from the Earth | The excessive brightness of the Moon
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the object designated Sagittarius A*. | It is a black hole at the centre of the galaxy, first imaged in 2022 | It is a bright star in the constellation Sagittarius | It is a small galaxy near the Milky Way | It is a planet orbiting a distant star
